emuvdi - the EmuTOS VDI, built for the host
===========================================

tosemu translates TOS calls rather than emulating a machine, and the VDI is far
too large a thing to write again when a free one already exists. EmuTOS carries
a VDI descended from the GEM sources Caldera released under the GPL in 1999,
and it is the same licence as tosemu.

It arrives as a submodule, 3rdparty/emutos, pinned to VERSION_1_4. The
submodule is read only: nothing in it is edited, and everything that adapts it
lives here. That is the opposite of how Musashi is carried, which is vendored
and patched, with the patch invisible in the tree.


What is used
------------

The drawing files, compiled as they stand, about ten thousand lines:

    vdi_control.c vdi_line.c vdi_raster.c vdi_textblit.c vdi_text.c
    vdi_fill.c vdi_col.c vdi_bezier.c vdi_gdp.c vdi_marker.c vdi_misc.c
    util/intmath.c

and the Atari ST system fonts, bios/fnt_st_*.c with their offset tables.

Not used, because they are the parts a hosted VDI has to answer for itself:

    vdi_main.c                 nothing: it is the freestanding boot, but the
                               dispatcher in it is wanted, so it is built
    vdi_mouse.c vdi_input.c    the pointer and the input, which belong to the
                               compositor
    vdi_esc.c                  terminal escapes
    every .S                   see below

vdi_control.c is the workstation management - opening, closing and clearing
them, and vq_extnd - rather than the trap entry, which is vdi_main.c. Both are
built. screen() in vdi_main.c reads the control array, finds the workstation
the handle names, fills in how many answers there are and calls the right
function, so tosemu does not dispatch VDI calls at all: it copies the arrays
out of the emulated memory and hands them over whole. See emuvdi.h and
bridge.c, which are the only things on both sides.

vdi_mouse.c stays out. EmuTOS draws a pointer into the screen itself, saving
and restoring what it covers, because on an ST there is nothing else to draw
one; here there will be a compositor with a pointer of its own. The calls
screen() dispatches to still have to exist, so hostvars.c stands in for them.
v_show_c and v_hide_c are honest as they are. vsc_form, which sets the shape,
will have to say something to the compositor once there is one.


How it is adapted
-----------------

Three levers, no edits.

1. Include order. asm.h and intmath.h here come first on the include path, so
   the EmuTOS sources reach these rather than EmuTOS's own. The originals are
   m68k inline assembly; every macro in them has a portable meaning.

   Shadowing intmath.h means providing min, max and Isqrt as well, since
   EmuTOS keeps them there. Isqrt itself is portable C and is compiled from
   the submodule.

2. biosbind.h and xbiosbind.h, likewise shadowed. The originals are trap
   instructions. The VDI reaches the BIOS only to hang its timer routine on a
   vector, and the XBIOS only to set and read palette registers.

3. -D__mcoldfire__, which is the interesting one. EmuTOS uses it as its
   "target without m68k inline assembly" switch, and all seven uses of it in
   the files above mean exactly that. It is what selects the C rotate macros,
   the C bit_blt in vdi_raster.c, and the C loops in vdi_line.c, and it sets
   ASM_BLIT_IS_AVAILABLE to 0 so that none of the assembly is wanted. That is
   why no .S file is built and why no file here is a copy of one there.

   The claim is not true of the host in any other sense, so the config knobs
   it would otherwise flip are pinned explicitly in the Makefile rather than
   left to follow from it. All of them concern hardware that is not there:
   the MFP timer, ColdFire RS232, cache control, the PMMU, CPU detection.

CONF_WITH_VDI_TEXT_SPEEDUP is off, and for a different reason from the rest.
It turns on direct_screen_blit, which draws a byte at a time and chooses which
byte of a word by testing bit 3 of the x coordinate. That is the right byte
only on a big endian machine. Surfaces here are host endian, which is what lets
the rest of the VDI go unedited, so the two halves of every word are the other
way round and a glyph lands in the wrong one. With it off, all text goes
through normal_blit, which works in words and does not care.

Should a file ever genuinely need editing, it is copied into this directory and
compiled in place of the submodule's, with a header saying which revision it
came from and what changed. That list is currently empty. If it reaches three
or so, fork EmuTOS instead and point the submodule at the fork, so the changes
get a history and can go back upstream.


What is here
------------

    asm.h        the m68k inline assembly of EmuTOS's asm.h, in C
    intmath.h    the same for intmath.h, plus min, max and Isqrt
    biosbind.h   the one BIOS call the VDI makes
    xbiosbind.h  the palette and resolution calls the VDI makes
    bdosbind.h   where a virtual workstation's memory comes from. Not the
                 shape EmuTOS declares: its Mxalloc answers with a LONG, which
                 cannot hold a pointer on a 64 bit host, and the caller casts
                 that answer straight to a Vwk *
    hostvars.c   the globals the VDI draws through, which EmuTOS keeps in
                 assembly because line-A needs them at fixed offsets
    fonts.c      the three ST system fonts, taken directly rather than through
                 EmuTOS's bios/font.c, which asks country.c which fonts a
                 machine sold in a given country should have
    textblit.c   normal_blit, the one part of the VDI written here rather than
                 adapted - see below
    vditest.c    draws into a surface and prints it, so that the port is
                 checked by looking at pixels rather than at whether it built


normal_blit
-----------

This is the only file here that replaces EmuTOS code rather than adapting it.
vdi_textblit.c calls normal_blit to put a character on the screen, and EmuTOS
has it in vdi_tblit.S and a ColdFire variant of that, with no C behind either.
The other three routines the same comment calls assembler - outline, rotate and
scale - do have C definitions in vdi_textblit.c, despite what it says.

The original works a word at a time, with fringe masks at each end and four
variants of the loop. This works a pixel at a time, which is slower by a large
factor and does not matter: a glyph is eight by sixteen. What is bought with
the cycles is that the loop variants and the rotate direction stop existing.

Two things that are not optional, both found by drawing and looking:

The effect masks come from the globals LITEMASK and SKEWMASK, not from the
lite_msk and skew_msk fields. vdi_textblit.c never writes those fields: the
assembly reloads them itself at the top of each character, so they hold
whatever the character before left behind.

Reads have to be clamped to the glyph. A font is a strip, so the bits either
side of a character are its neighbours rather than blank, and skew reads to the
left of the glyph. Without the clamp the lean is drawn out of whichever
character happens to precede it, which is what the fringe masks were for.


Pointers in thirty two bit fields
---------------------------------

GEM keeps addresses in LONGs. An OBJECT's ob_spec is a LONG holding the address
of a string; a USERBLK holds the address of a routine; an MFDB holds the
address of a bitmap. That is not something the code can be talked out of - it
is the layout applications and resource files are built to, and it is the same
on both sides of the trap.

So every address this side of tosemu puts in one has to fit in thirty two bits.
The whole program is therefore linked without position independence: it lands
at 0x400000 and its static data with it, well under the four gigabyte line.
Memory allocated as it runs is asked for below the line explicitly, in
host_vdi_alloc, because a large malloc is served by mmap and would otherwise
land wherever the kernel liked - and a resource file is exactly the sort of
thing that is large.

This is worth knowing because getting it wrong does not crash. A position
independent build puts static data above 0x550000000000; truncating one of
those to a LONG leaves an address that is usually still mapped, so it draws
rubbish rather than stopping. The object tree in vditest rendered correctly
under a PIE build by luck, and would not have on the next run.


The AES
-------

The library files of EmuTOS's AES - the object, form, resource, graphics and
rectangle ones, and gemgsxif.c, which is its VDI interface - are built here
too. All they do is call the VDI, which is why they come across.

The kernel does not. EmuTOS's is cooperative multitasking with every
application in one address space, and tosemu runs an application to a process,
so appl_, evnt_, wind_, menu_ and shel_ are written rather than taken.
aeskernel.c is the seam: everything the library files reach down for, arrived
at by compiling them and seeing what was left over. Nothing in it is
implemented yet, and what is reached says so.

gsx2.c is replaced. The original is thirty three lines that point a parameter
block at the control array and execute a trap, and that one instruction is the
whole path between the two halves of GEM. Here both are host code, so it is a
call, and nothing is copied because there is nothing to copy between.

Four files are copied and edited, which is the whole override list. Three of
the four are the same bug in different places: EmuTOS says a LONG is a signed
32 bit word and writes it as long, which is four bytes on a 68000 and eight
here. Anything that then treats a LONG as a fixed number of bytes, or reaches
into one by position, finds the wrong thing.

An OBJECT is 24 bytes in the machine and 32 in this program for the same
reason, which is why aestree.c does not copy a tree as a block: it reads the
fields at the machine's offsets and hands them to emuvdi.h to be put wherever
this side keeps them. Nothing outside emuvdi/ knows either layout.

gemoblib.c: call_usercode in it is fifteen lines of assembly that jsr to a
routine the application supplied, and here that routine is 68000 code in the
emulated machine.

gemobjop.c: a box's border thickness is the second byte of its ob_spec, and
EmuTOS takes it by pointing a char at the LONG and stepping one along. That
finds it where a LONG is four bytes and the high one comes first. Here it is
eight bytes and the low one comes first, so the step lands in the middle of the
colour word and a two pixel border is drawn seventeen pixels thick. Shifting
says which byte is wanted rather than where it sits, and is right either way.

vdi_raster.c: a bitmap's address arrives in two words of the control array and
is read back as *(MFDB **)&CONTRL[7]. On a 68000 that reads four bytes, the
whole address. On a sixty four bit host it reads eight, and since the two
bitmaps a call names are only two words apart, the source read swallows the
destination address as its high half. Four reads now take thirty two bits,
which is what the caller wrote - the AES stores them through ULONG_AT, which
is thirty two bits wide, and so was already doing it that way.


Things that bite
----------------

get_start_addr, which turns a pixel column into an address, lives in
vdi_misc.c beside the timer code. That file is wanted for the one and not the
other, which is why it is built rather than replaced.

mul_div_round rounds by doubling, adding or subtracting one, and arithmetic
shifting. A divide is not the same function: it truncates towards zero where a
shift goes towards minus infinity.

The drawing helpers assume the VDI entry point ran first. polyline draws
nothing at all unless LN_MASK holds a line style, which draw_pline is what
normally sets. A handler that calls a helper directly has to set up what the
entry point would have, and getting it wrong is silent.

textblit.c copies the LOCALVARS structure, which is private to vdi_textblit.c.
It is the interface between the two, so it has to follow any change made there
- the same warning the original carries about the assembly.

Half of what EMUTOSFLAGS does is choose between paths inside EmuTOS rather than
how to compile them, so a changed flag changes the program. The objects depend
on the Makefile for that reason. Without it, changing a flag leaves stale
objects that look like a bug in the code.
